d it lost 4.1 points: the lead went from -5.8 to -1.7. The engine wanted H3 instead, so Black was handed a way back that it did not yet use."}, {"turn": 15, "text": "Opus 5.5 answered with H4, which lost 3.4 points and sent the lead from -1.7 back to -5.1. The engine's choice was G6, next to White's stones. The 4.1-point gift from the previous move was gone at once."}, {"turn": 24, "text": "A7 by the 14k lost 4.8 points, the worst move of White's game, moving the lead from -4.7 to +0.2. The engine preferred J4 on the right side. This was the first time Black was ahead, and it happened on the left edge, not in the centre."}, {"turn": 27, "text": "Opus 5.5 played A4 and lost 4.6 points, its worst move, as the lead dropped from +0.6 to -4.0. The engine wanted J5. The left-edge fight traded the lead back and forth, with both sides losing around 4 to 5 points on nearly every move from 24 to 28."}, {"turn": 29, "text": "Opus 5.5 found J5, the engine's own choice, and the lead went from +0.1 to +0.1. After the left-edge swings the board was quiet, and every move from here to the end lost 0.3 points or less, which kept the result a draw."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "after.py places one move the player names, removes captured stones, and prints each group's liberties; libs.py only prints group liberties for a pasted board. Neither loops over candidate moves, scores them, or plays any continuation, so they only check the effect of a single named move on the current position.", "stop": false}}